CORE
UserGranularityCheckAdder
--function "privacy.UserGranularityCheckAdder.validate:(Lprivacy/ColumnAttributes;)V" --jml-source src/main/kotlin/privacy/UserGranularityCheckAdder.kt --unwind 3 -cp target/classes
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR
^.*FAILURE$
--
End-to-end smoke test that mirrors the AWS-Bastion privacy
pipeline's `validateNonViewableColumns` shape — chained
trivial getters, requires/ensures gated by exception state,
typed signals, signals_only, and assignable \nothing — all
on Kotlin source.

The class shape is deliberately close to the production
UserGranularityCheckAdder.kt:

  data class Sensitivity(val viewable: Boolean)
  data class ColumnAttributes(val sensitivity: Sensitivity)

with the JBMC-verifiable subset of the contract:

  //@ requires attrs != null;
  //@ requires attrs.getSensitivity() != null;
  //@ assignable \nothing;
  //@ ensures attrs.getSensitivity().getViewable();
  //@ signals_only InvalidInputException;
  //@ signals (InvalidInputException e) e != null;

This exercises five of the JBMC features added in the
java-contracts feature branch:
  * trivial bean-getter recognition (Kotlin val accessors
    becoming JVM-style getter methods);
  * chained getter-call resolution
    (attrs.getSensitivity().getViewable() rewrites to a
    nested member_exprt against the backing fields);
  * inline (non-modular) `assignable` enforcement;
  * exception-gated `ensures`;
  * typed signals (T e) p exit-time assertion.

What the production code uses that's not yet supported here
is `Map<ColumnVertex, ColumnAttributes>` — JBMC needs
java.util.Map library models for that. The smoke test uses
a single-column shape to sidestep the Map dependency while
preserving every other AWS-Bastion idiom.

The full Brazil-built bytecode of the production
UserGranularityCheckAdder.kt would verify with the same
flags once Map model support lands.
